 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are delivered to connected browsers over self-hosted WebSockets, giving fast updates without a recurring realtime-vendor fee or provider coupling. This exceeds the 15-second freshness goal; subsecond delivery is a pleasant consequence, not a target in itself.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Self-hosting sockets means the board owns connection capacity and reconnect behavior, so the change store must keep replayable revision history, delivery must manage socket sessions and publish committed revisions reliably, and browsers must reconnect and catch up missed revisions from the store. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected as the default route: it meets the freshness goal with the simplest stateless operation, but repeats reads continuously and lags a few seconds behind commits. A hosted realtime service was rejected: it reaches low latency with less connection operations work, but adds recurring fees and couples the board's core update path to a vendor.
 
